;;; Evaluation.mon --- Orthogonal evaluation strategies for recursive structure

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.2.0
:keywords evaluation strategy strict lazy suspension modality demand totality evidence

;;; Commentary:

 Evaluation is the Core boundary between what a value is and when recursive
 work needed to obtain that value is performed.

 Structural abstractions such as Sequence, Tree, Graph, Trie, and future user
 structures must not duplicate strict and lazy variants of their semantics.
 They describe shape. An Evaluation instance describes the operational policy
 used at recursive positions. In the intended architecture:

   Sequence + Strict = strict sequence
   Sequence + Lazy   = lazy sequence
   Tree     + Strict = strict tree
   Tree     + Lazy   = lazy tree.

 The class is intentionally tiny. A strategy receives a suspended pure
 computation as a nullary callback and decides when that callback is executed.
 Passing a callback rather than an eager value is essential: in an eager host
 language, an ordinary =defer value= could not implement call-by-need because
 VALUE would already have been evaluated before the method was called.

;;; Semantic Model

 For each evaluation strategy E, think of E as an endofunctor on the category
 of pure Monad values. Core exposes

                 defer_E  : (1 -> A) -> E A
                 demand_E : E A -> A.

 Extensionally, demand is a retraction of defer:

                 demand_E (defer_E k)  ≃  k ().

 Here ≃ is equality of the resulting pure value, not equality of evaluation
 trace, allocation, or sharing. Those intensional properties distinguish
 Strict from Lazy and are specified by their owning modules.

 The observer =demand= respects Applicative structure. In categorical language
 it is an applicative natural transformation from E to the identity functor:

        demand_E (pure x)       ≃  x
        demand_E (map f x)      ≃  f (demand_E x)
        demand_E (liftA2 f x y) ≃  f (demand_E x) (demand_E y).

 Evaluation deliberately does not claim Monad, Comonad, idempotence, or a
 proof-theoretic modal structure. Those stronger structures require their own
 laws and must not be inferred merely from strict/lazy timing.

 =pure= does not replace =defer=. In an eager language the argument to =pure e=
 is evaluated before pure is called, whereas defer receives a closure whose
 body the strategy may execute now or later. Applicative describes combination
 of strategy values; defer describes evaluation timing.

;;; Total Demand Evidence

 =E A= alone does not imply that demanding the value terminates. This matters
 most for Lazy, where an unrestricted thunk may contain pure divergence.

 =TotalDemand E A= is an opaque refinement of =E A= carrying the proposition

                    demand_E value terminates : A.

 The wrapper is proof-relevant to the type checker but representation-irrelevant
 to execution. Its constructor is intentionally private. Ordinary Core can
 introduce evidence through =total-defer=: the callback has the plain total
 arrow, so Evaluation's beta law proves the resulting demand total. =pure= is
 total for the same reason. Functor and Applicative preserve the evidence when
 their callbacks are themselves total.

                    TotalDemand E A  ->  E A
                    TotalDemand E A  ->  A

 are eliminations. The first forgets the proof. The second demands the value
 without introducing the =diverge= effect.

 This is deliberately weaker than a guarded-recursion later modality. It proves
 one concrete demand total; it does not by itself prove an infinite recursive
 object productive. Recursive productivity is obtained when a representation
 places TotalDemand evidence at every continuation produced by its coalgebra.

 The distinction is crucial for value-level reasoning. =Lazy A= contains both
 terminating and diverging thunks, while =TotalDemand Lazy A= selects only the
 former without pretending that every Lazy value is total.

;;; Effects and Quantities

 The callback accepted by defer has no externally observable effects. It may be
 total or may carry only the distinguished =diverge= effect; IO, mutation,
 exceptions, foreign calls, allocation with observable identity, and similar
 effects are excluded. Changing Strict to Lazy must never change the
 multiplicity of an observable effect. A future effectful suspension belongs to
 a distinct abstraction with explicit sharing semantics.

 The compact signatures below cannot yet spell the difference between a total
 callback and one carrying =diverge=. Normatively, =total-defer= accepts only the
 former. The ordinary =defer= may accept either. This distinction is part of the
 higher-order effect contract the compiler must implement.

 Evaluation also respects Monad's quantitative type theory. A deferred
 computation captures its environment with the quantities inferred for that
 closure. A strategy may not duplicate a quantity-1 capture merely because it
 stores or memoizes the closure. TotalDemand adds proof, never duplicability.
 The quantity of the underlying evaluation value remains authoritative.

;;; Research Basis

 John Launchbury, "A Natural Semantics for Lazy Evaluation", POPL 1993.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/158511.158618

 Zena Ariola and Matthias Felleisen, "The Call-by-Need Lambda Calculus",
 Journal of Functional Programming, 1997.
   https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796898003037

 Paul Blain Levy, "Call-by-Push-Value: A Subsuming Paradigm", TLCA 1999.
   https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48959-2_17

 Hiroshi Nakano, "A Modality for Recursion", LICS 2000.
   https://doi.org/10.1109/LICS.2000.855774

 Robert Atkey and Conor McBride, "Productive Coprogramming with Guarded
 Recursion", ICFP 2013.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/2500365.2500597

 Aleš Bizjak, Hans Bugge Grathwohl, Ranald Clouston, Rasmus E. Møgelberg, and
 Lars Birkedal, "Guarded Dependent Type Theory with Coinductive Types", 2016.
   https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-49630-5_2

 Operational call-by-need motivates Evaluation. Guarded-recursion work motivates
 compositional productivity evidence, while also showing why unrestricted Lazy
 must not simply be identified with the logical later modality. TotalDemand is
 the operational refinement at exactly that boundary.

;;; Code:

import Control.Applicative
import Data.Functor
import Data.Bool

module Control.Evaluation
  [Evaluation defer demand
   TotalDemand total-defer total-pure forget-total demand-total]


class Applicative e => Evaluation e where
  defer  :: (() -> a) -> e a
  demand :: e a -> a

  ;; Minimal complete definition: defer and demand.
  ;;
  ;; The equations in Commentary are meta-theoretic laws. They are deliberately
  ;; not executable laws yet: evaluating both sides could duplicate quantity-1
  ;; captures. A later proof language may quantify over unrestricted values.


data TotalDemand e a
  = TotalDemandValue (e a)


;; Normatively THUNK has an empty effect row, including no diverge
define total-defer :: Evaluation e => (() -> a) -> TotalDemand e a
  thunk -> TotalDemandValue (defer thunk)


define total-pure :: Evaluation e => a -> TotalDemand e a
  value -> TotalDemandValue (pure value)


define forget-total :: TotalDemand e a -> e a
  [TotalDemandValue value] -> value


| Normatively this elimination is total because the stored evidence discharges
| the latent divergence of demand.
define demand-total :: Evaluation e => TotalDemand e a -> a
  [TotalDemandValue value] -> demand value


instance Evaluation e => Functor (TotalDemand e)
  map f [TotalDemandValue value] -> TotalDemandValue (map f value)


instance Evaluation e => Applicative (TotalDemand e)
  pure value -> total-pure value
  applyA [TotalDemandValue f] [TotalDemandValue value] ->
    TotalDemandValue (applyA f value)
